From 27 July to 4 August 2026, six members of the STORIES project — **Prof. Dr. Liang Emlyn Yang, Dr. Ylenia Casali, Ms. Siying Chen, Ms. Pia Echtler, Mr. Thanh Phuoc Ho, and Mr. Hao Su** — conducted fieldwork across two Thai regions: **Ubon Ratchathani province** along the Mekong River in northeastern Thailand, and **Samut Sakhon province** located in the coastal area south of Bangkok.
The fieldwork was in collaboration with researchers of the **Chulalongkorn University Social Research Institute (CUSRI)** in Bangkok, led by Prof. Unruan Leknoi. By combining household surveys, interviews with community representatives and government officials, and field observations, the research team explored how communities experience and respond to different environmental risks, ranging from river flooding and changing river ecosystems to coastal erosion and mangrove degradation.Overall, this work experience was immersive to learn from Thai communities what their problems and adaptive measures are in the face of flooding, erosion, and related ecosystem changes. Working in a different cultural and environmental context also provided a priceless life experience. Indeed, direct contact with local communities and places bridged the gap between theory and empirical reality, while also providing an opportunity to reflect on our personal perspectives while exchanging with others.

# Training for the Survey Team 
  Before starting the household survey, STORIES members conducted a **training session with the CUSRI interview team.** The questionnaire was reviewed together to ensure a shared understanding of its structure and research objectives.
The session also provided an opportunity to discuss how questions could be communicated clearly in the local context and how interviews could be conducted consistently across different communities. Through active discussion between researchers and local interviewers, practical experiences and local perspectives were incorporated into the survey process. This exchange helped establish a common approach for the subsequent household interviews and strengthened collaboration between the two research teams.

